 to them.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2602.07297v1] Progressive Searching for Retrieval in RAG (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07297v1)\nciteturn31568view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31560view3\",\"lineno\":16}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Information Retrieval\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 7 Feb 2026]\nL9: # Title:Progressive Searching for Retrieval in RAG\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Taehee Jeong , cite6†Xingzhe Zhao , cite7†Peizu Li , cite8†Markus Valvur , cite9†Weihua Zhao L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Progressive Searching for Retrieval in RAG, by Taehee Jeong and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) is a promising technique for mitigating two key limitations of large language models (LLMs): outdated information and hallucinations. RAG system stores documents as embedding vectors in a database. Given a query, search is executed to find the most related documents. Then, the topmost matching documents are inserted into LLMs' prompt to generate a response. Efficient and accurate searching is critical for RAG to get relevant information.\nL17: We propose a cost-effective searching algorithm for retrieval process. Our progressive searching algorithm incrementally refines the candidate set through a hierarchy of searches, starting from low-dimensional embeddings and progressing into a higher, target-dimensionality. This multi-stage approach reduces retrieval time while preserving the desired accuracy.\nL18: Our findings demonstrate that progressive search in RAG systems achieves a balance between dimensionality, speed, and accuracy, enabling scalable and high-performance retrieval even for large databases.\nL19: Subjects:  | Information Retrieval (cs.IR); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2602.07297 [cs.IR]\nL21:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2602.07297v1 [cs.IR] for this version)\nL22:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.07297†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Taehee Jeong [cite15†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Sat, 7 Feb 2026 01:12:53 UTC (656 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Progressive Searching for Retrieval in RAG, by Taehee Jeong and 4 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite10†View PDF L35:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite16†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite17†view license†creativecommons.org L39: \nL40: ### Additional Features\nL41: \nL42:   * cite18†Audio Summary L43: ### Current browse context:\nL44: \nL45: cs.IR\nL46: \nL47: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L48: \nL49: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2026-02 L50: \nL51: Change to browse by:\nL52: \nL53: cite24†cs L54: cite25†cs.AI L55: \nL56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L59:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L60:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L61: \nL62: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL63: \nL64: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL65: \nL66: [Button: ×]\nL67: \nL68: loading...\nL69: \nL70: Data provided by:\nL71: \nL72: ### Bookmark\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL75: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL78: \nL79: Bibliographic Explorer (cite29†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL80: \nL81: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL82: \nL83: Connected Papers (cite30†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpap